Published literature offers a valuable data source for HEA research, which is accessible through manual collections and automated text mining (TM). Traditional manual collections offer researchers direct control and a deep understanding of the data but can be subjective, time-consuming, and limited in scope. It often suffers from a lower information density due to the inherent limitations of manual processing [ 187 ]. TM, leveraging natural language processing (NLP) techniques, provides a powerful alternative. These structured and repeatable workflows can analyze vast quantities of text, extracting critical information for various purposes [ 188 ]. For example, Pei et al. [ 189 ] used TM and ML techniques to identify potential HEA compositions from a large amount of scientific literature and alloy data. They screened nearly 500 HEA candidates from 2.6 million alloys by analyzing the context-similarity of the elements based on a word-embedding model (skip-gram, shown in Fig.  4 a). This context-similarity approach reflects researchers’ usage of elements in scientific literature, leveraging co-occurrence patterns to quantify elemental associations and predict mutual solubility in alloy formation. For an  N -component HEA, the context-similarity,  S ij \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
