Several methods exist for identifying outliers, such as interquartile range (IQR), winsorizing, trimming, standard deviation, sample Mahalanobis distance squared, Wilks’ statistic, leverage, residuals, standardized residuals, studentized deleted residuals (external studentized residuals), difference in the fitted value (DfFit), and Cook’s distance [ 214 ]. Commonly used methods include the standard-deviation method and the IQR method. The standard-deviation method typically defines an outlier as a value more than three standard deviations from the mean. As a univariate technique, it does not account for rare cases arising from variable correlations. Furthermore, this method is susceptible to a masking effect, as outliers tend to inflate the standard deviation and influence the mean, thus reducing their standardized values [ 214 ]. The IQR method identifies outliers as data points outside a defined boundary region. This boundary is calculated using the dataset’s 25 th percentile (Q1) and 75 th percentile (Q3). The IQR is Q3 − Q1. The lower limit is designated as Q1 − 1.5 × IQR, and the upper limit is Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Points below the lower limit or above the upper limit are considered outliers [ 215 ]. The ordinary least squares (OLS) regression [ 216 ] and Pearson’s correlation coefficient (PCC) [ 217 ] are commonly used to address the impact of outliers on statistical inference. OLS is based on minimizing the sum of squared deviations from the conditional mean [ 214 ]. PCC is defined as follows [ 217 ,  218 ]: 8 r xy = 1 n - 1 ∑ i = 1 n x i - x ¯ y i - y ¯ s x s y \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
